  <section>
  <h2>What is GPS?</h2>

  <figure>
    <img class="intext-image" src="img/7414657260_a6288507a4_o.jpg" alt="A GPS Satellite">
  </figure>

  <p class="intro">
    GPS is a satellite-based system that helps devices find their location.
    GPS receivers use software algorithms to process satellite signal times and find a device's location, speed, and direction.
    GPS is an integral part of modern life, used for navigation, mapping, and timing in various applications.
    It is maintained by the United States government and is freely available to anyone with a GPS receiver.
  </p>
  
  <details>
    <summary>GPS Segments</summary>
    <p>
      The GPS system has three main parts- the space segment, the control segment, and the user segment. 
      The space segment consists of 24 satellites transmitting one-way signals giving current GPS satellite position and time.
      The control segment are the monitor and control stations maintaining satellite health and orbit.
      The user segment includes GPS receivers, which can be found in phones, cars, and other devices.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>GPS Services</summary>
    <p>
      GPS services are used by both military and civilian users for various applications, such as navigation, mapping, and timing synchronization.
      Various GPS augmentation systems and techniques can enhance system performance, including accuracy, integrity, and availability.
      When you use a map application on your phone, you are using GPS services and the map's software to determine your location and provide directions.
      Algorithms in the software can determine the optimal route to your destination based on your current location and the map data.
    </p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>The Future of GPS</summary>
    <p>
      As technology advances, GPS is expected to become even more accurate and reliable. 
      The United States plan to implement features to support safety of life applications in aviation, maritime, and other critical sectors.
    </p>
  </details>
